  is unrelated to this session's LWSContext additions (cookie jar, conmon,
  retry, resolve, schedule). gdb backtrace bottoms out inside
  __lws_close_free_wsi() (libwebsockets, no further symbols available)
  called from lwsjs_socket_close(), itself called via a promise reaction
  job resuming an async test function - i.e. a wsi is being closed from
  JS during/after some other async continuation, and something about that
  ordering corrupts or double-frees wsi state. Not investigated further
  (out of scope - this session didn't touch lws-socket.c's close path or
  tcpsocket.js/tcpsocketstream.js).

    (run: QUICKJS_MODULE_PATH="build/x86_64-linux-debug;." qjsm
    tests/unittests/test-tcpsocket.js)

- response-ok-stale-after-establish (lib/lws/response.js:35, Response
  constructor; lib/lws/protocols.js:290, HttpClientProtocol#onEstablishedClientHttp):
  `resp.ok` is computed once from `options.status` at construction time
  (defaulting to 200 when the caller didn't pass a status, which
  HttpClientProtocol never does), but the real HTTP status only gets
  patched onto `resp.status` later, in onEstablishedClientHttp - `resp.ok`
  is never recomputed, so it reads `true` for every client HTTP response
  regardless of the actual status code. Found while building
  examples/ollama-repl (its ollama-client.js checks `resp.status` directly
  instead, with a comment explaining why). fetch() (lib/fetch.js) has the
  same exposure for any caller checking `response.ok`.

    const resp = await fetch('http://localhost:1/nope-should-404-or-refuse');
    console.log(resp.ok); // true, even though resp.status is not 2xx

- tostring-silently-undefined-on-typed-array-view (js-utils.c:266,
  get_buffer; lws.c:322, FUNCTION_TO_STRING): toString(v) from lws.so
  returns undefined instead of the decoded string when `v` is a typed
  array *view* (e.g. Uint8Array) rather than a plain ArrayBuffer - no
  throw, no warning, just undefined. Root cause: get_buffer() calls
  JS_GetArrayBuffer(), which (per quickjs.h) only accepts an ArrayBuffer;
  a TypedArray view needs JS_GetTypedArrayBuffer() instead, which
  get_buffer() never tries. get_buffer()'s own doc comment already
  documents "returns NULL, caller falls back" as intentional for the
  non-ArrayBuffer case generally (e.g. LWSSockAddr46's constructor probing
  for an ArrayBuffer vs a numeric-address string) - so this may be
  deliberate scope there, but it silently breaks any caller that expected
  toString()/get_buffer() to accept the Uint8Array views this codebase
  hands out routinely (e.g. ReadableStream chunks - see
  readableStreamCallback() usage in lib/lws/protocols.js's
  onReceiveClientHttpRead, which wraps every chunk in `new Uint8Array(...)`
  before enqueueing it). Found via examples/ollama-repl's chatStream()
  (lib/ollama-client.js), which now works around it by slicing out the
  view's backing ArrayBuffer region before calling toString().

    import { toString } from 'lws.so';
    console.log(toString(new Uint8Array([72, 105])));       // undefined
    console.log(toString(new Uint8Array([72, 105]).buffer)); // "Hi"

- reassigned-const-detached-buffer-helpers (lib/lws/streams.js:399,410):
  TransferArrayBuffer and IsDetachedBuffer are declared with `const` but
  their own bodies self-reassign (`TransferArrayBuffer = buffer => ...`)
  as a one-time memoized-strategy trick. In strict/module mode this throws
  "'IsDetachedBuffer' is read-only" the first time either is called, which
  happens on any byte-mode (`type: 'bytes'`) ReadableStream enqueue via
  ReadableByteStreamControllerEnqueue. Predates the CTRL/STRM/READ/WRITE
  hidden-props migration (commit f7646ad) - same bug exists at the
  pre-migration commit (505e1e8). Found while manually exercising BYOB
  reader paths, which aren't covered by tests/unittests/test-streams.js.

    import { ReadableStream } from './lib/lws/streams.js';
    new ReadableStream({
      type: 'bytes',
      start(controller) { controller.enqueue(new Uint8Array([1, 2, 3])); },
    });

- console-error-kills-process (native console.error binding, exercised via
  quickjs qjs binary in build/x86_64-linux-debug/qjs): calling
  console.error(...) silently terminates the script with exit code 0 - no
  output, no exception surfaced, nothing after it runs. Confirmed with a
  two-line repro outside streams.js entirely, so it's an engine/binding
  issue, not an application bug. This is what made tests/unittests/test-
  streams.js look like it was hanging/crashing on failing tests: tinytest.js
  calls console.error() to report a failure, which killed the process
  before it could print "FAILED" or continue to the next test - a passing
  suite is silent about this, but any future failing test will look like a
  silent hang again unless this is fixed.

    console.log('a');
    console.error('this line kills the process');
    console.log('b'); // never printed, exit code is still 0

- tinytest-always-prints-ok (tests/unittests/tinytest.js:50,56):
  TinyTest.run() prints "Test: <name> OK" twice for every passing test (once
  inside the try block, once again unconditionally right after the
  try/catch), and prints it even for a test whose testAction() threw and
  hit the catch block - the catch path increments `failures` and logs
  "FAILED", but execution falls through to the same unconditional
  console.log('Test:', testName, 'OK') afterward, so a failing run's output
  reads as passing unless you're also watching the failures/count summary
  line. Combined with console-error-kills-process above (which fires from
  the same catch block right before this), a failing test currently kills
  the process before this masking line even runs, but if that bug gets
  fixed independently this one will start producing misleading "OK" output
  for failed tests. Found while investigating why the streams.js migration
  bugs weren't visibly failing in the test-streams.js run.

    import { tests } from './tests/unittests/tinytest.js';
    await tests({ 'always looks ok'() { throw new Error('nope'); } });
    // prints "Test: always looks ok FAILED ..." AND "Test: always looks ok OK"
